# Four DGX Sparks, one rank each. `./glm53 init`, run on one of the Sparks, writes `hosts` (git-ignored) for you: it
# finds the other three on the ConnectX-7 fabric (`--hosts H0,H1,H2,H3` names them instead). By hand, copy this file
# and edit it:
#   cp tensorfold-four-spark-tp4/hosts.example tensorfold-four-spark-tp4/hosts
#
# columns: rank  ssh_target  fabric_ip  [peer_ssh]
#   rank        0..3. Rank 0 serves the /v1 API and is the rendezvous master; ranks 1-3 start first.
#   ssh_target  how serve.sh reaches the Spark from where you run it (an ~/.ssh/config alias, user@host, an IP, or
#               `local` for the machine serve.sh runs on). `./glm53 init` writes `local` for rank 0 and the fabric
#               IPs for ranks 1-3.
#   fabric_ip   the Spark's IPv4 address on the ConnectX-7 port (FABRIC_IFNAME: init records the port it found in
#               cluster.env; env.sh's default is enp1s0f0np0). NCCL, the RoCE
#               setup and the rendezvous use it; tools/b12x_env.sh resolves the RoCE v2 GID of this address.
#   peer_ssh    optional: how rank 0's watchdog reaches this rank over ssh (default: fabric_ip). Rank 0 must be able
#               to `ssh -o BatchMode=yes <peer_ssh>` ranks 1-3 without a password: it stops all four on a breach.
0  spark-0  10.0.0.10
1  spark-1  10.0.0.11
2  spark-2  10.0.0.12
3  spark-3  10.0.0.13
